// Package fio holds the two whole-stream helpers `pkg/std/io` deliberately
// leaves out: read everything, and copy everything.
//
// Both take INTERFACES (`@io.Reader`, `@io.Writer`), not `@os.File`, which is
// the point of the io package existing at all: nothing here knows what a file
// is, so the same code serves a file, a `strings.Builder`, or the fake streams
// in fio_test.bn — and the tests need no filesystem.
//
// Both also encode the Reader contract that trips people up: a Read may deliver
// bytes AND fail in the same call, so `n` is honored before the error is
// inspected. End of input is not a failure — it arrives as `io.EOF`, which these
// helpers absorb (`io.IsEOF`) and report as success.
package "pkg/fio"

import "pkg/std/errors"
import "pkg/std/io"

// ChunkSize is how many bytes ReadAll and Copy move per underlying Read. Any
// size is correct — a Reader may return fewer bytes than asked for at any time —
// so this trades syscalls against buffer size, nothing else.
const ChunkSize int = 4096

// ReadAll reads r until end of input and returns everything it produced. The
// result is `@[]readonly char`: rendered text the caller owns and reads, which is
// what a `strings.Builder` hands back.
//
// On failure it returns the bytes read so far along with the error — a partial
// read is usually more useful than nothing, and the caller can tell the cases
// apart with `present(err)`.
func ReadAll(r @io.Reader) (@[]readonly char, @errors.Error)

// Copy reads src until end of input, writing everything to dst, and returns the
// number of bytes written. A short write (dst accepting fewer bytes than offered)
// is a failure by io.Writer's contract, and Copy stops at the first one.
func Copy(dst @io.Writer, src @io.Reader) (int, @errors.Error)
